@* StatusLabel: the one shape the two closed status vocabularies wear (FR-18, FR-109, FR-117).

   A fill, a shape and a word. The shape is the part that earns the component: the design states
   that the green and the red "differ by shape and word; they are not colour-blind safe on hue
   alone", so every face carries a `::before` glyph drawn in `_theme.scss` as well as its tint. A
   screen that wrote `<span class="dt-status dt-status--delivered">` by hand would get the shape
   too - which is exactly why the class must not be spelled on a screen: the day a sixth face
   arrives, one component knows about it and nine screens do not.

   The word is the consumer's, and it stays there: the two faces of this label are
   DeliveryStatusLabel and ShiftStateLabel, each of which owns its own literal localizer keys so
   LocalizationTests can see them. This renders whatever they hand it, and decides only how it
   looks.

   The modifier is a total switch rather than a chain of @@if blocks, and that is the matrix row
   this closes: the label used to be four independent arms, so a status nobody had written an arm
   for rendered an empty span onto a table and a timeline. A face with no class now fails loudly
   here instead. *@

<span class="dt-status @Modifier @Class">@ChildContent</span>

@code {
    /// <summary>Which face to wear. A closed enum, so a typo is a build error.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    [EditorRequired]
    public DtStatusFace Face { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// The screen's own hook on this label, so a test can name one outcome among two. A hook only:
    /// a class passed in here carries this component's scope attribute rather than the screen's, so
    /// a screen-scoped rule written against it would match nothing at all. What the label looks like
    /// is <see cref="Face"/>'s to decide and this component's to draw.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string? Class { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The word, already localized by the component that owns the vocabulary.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public RenderFragment? ChildContent { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The modifier class the face wears.</summary>
    /// <exception cref="ArgumentOutOfRangeException">
    /// A face with no class. Total over the enum like <c>NavDestinations.For</c> and
    /// <c>ReviewViews.ClassFor</c>: a seventh face added without a decision about how it looks
    /// fails here rather than rendering an unlabelled, unshaped span.
    /// </exception>
    private string Modifier => Face switch
    {
        DtStatusFace.Pending => "dt-status--pending",
        DtStatusFace.Transit => "dt-status--transit",
        DtStatusFace.Delivered => "dt-status--delivered",
        DtStatusFace.Failed => "dt-status--failed",
        DtStatusFace.Running => "dt-status--running",
        DtStatusFace.Finished => "dt-status--finished",
        _ => throw new ArgumentOutOfRangeException(
            nameof(Face),
            Face,
            "No status class is defined for this face."),
    };
}
